= Speech act — Simple =

A '''speech act''' is something a person does by communicating.

When people use language, they can do more than describe facts. They can ask a question, make a promise, give a warning, apologize, request something, or make an announcement.

The grammatical form does not always tell you the action.

“Can you pass the salt?” looks like a question about ability, but people usually use it as a request.

[[Context (linguistics)/Simple|Context]] helps listeners understand what action the speaker is performing.

A speech act is also different from its effect. A warning is one act; whether it actually frightens or persuades someone is another matter.

